WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:19.580
Right now, you and I are watching a rotifer
just chill and do its rotifer thing.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:25.449
The long thin strands of cilia that make up
its crown are whirling around to grab food,

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:31.590
while the organisms around it are caught up and diverted by the vortex the rotifer has created.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:39.210
It’s a charming scene, as are so many of the other
scenes we’ve witnessed come out of the microcosmos.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.930
Whether you’re watching this at home or
at work, we’re all sharing in these strange

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:49.890
and delightful moments courtesy of our strange
and delightful microscopic friends.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.670
And we don’t mean to add a layer of existential
dread to the proceedings, but here’s something

00:00:54.670 --> 00:01:00.230
to think about: by the time you watch these
scenes, the stars of the show that come in

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:03.900
and out of view are most likely long gone.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:09.719
Perhaps they’ve been put back in the sample
jar, or maybe they’ve died on the slide.

00:01:09.719 --> 00:01:13.560
They may even have entered a protective cyst
state.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.610
Whatever path their lives have taken since
the moment they came into our view, we’ll

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:23.430
never be able to create these exact scenes
again.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:29.680
So we’re lucky today to have a way to preserve
and share their lives in video.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:35.460
And in the past, scientists turned to other
artistic means to document the microcosmos,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.220
including illustration.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:41.220
But sometimes, pictures and videos aren’t
enough.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:47.259
Sometimes the best way to share what you’ve
seen under the microscope is, well, to share

00:01:47.259 --> 00:01:50.830
the actual thing you’re looking at.

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:56.170
These slides that you’re looking at right
now were purchased from various auctions.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:02:03.060
Inside them are diatoms collected from around
the world, some from Rio de Janeiro, others

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.560
from England, and some from the Mediterranean
Sea.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.700
These are places we might not be able to visit
easily, and so who knows when we’ll be able

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.030
to collect some of their microscopic residents.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:22.060
But thanks to the fellow lovers of microscopes
and microbes out there who collect and preserve

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:29.129
their samples, we can actually see a bit of
what they’ve seen with our own microscopes.

00:02:29.129 --> 00:02:33.200
These prepared slides are meant to be permanent.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:39.080
The goal is to be able to return to them at
a later date, perhaps with fresh eyes or under

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.879
a new microscope.

00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:46.150
Maybe you’ve found a new species and want
to save it for later observation, or to convince

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.579
others that it’s truly real.

00:02:48.579 --> 00:02:53.819
Or maybe you’ve just found something that’s
beautiful or cool, something that you think

00:02:53.819 --> 00:02:56.760
others will want to see and learn from.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:01.349
Whatever the motivation, the results—as
you can see here—are often stunning.

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:07.189
Now there are many ways to prepare samples
for microscopic study because there are many

00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:12.779
different types of specimens to sample—from
microbes to pollen to organs.

00:03:12.779 --> 00:03:18.370
When we study our own samples here, we’re
using what’s called a “wet mount”—our

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:23.290
little buddies are swimming around in a liquid,
in this case water.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:28.529
We apply a bit of this watery sample to the
slide, enclose it with a coverslip, and then

00:03:28.529 --> 00:03:29.529
we observe.

00:03:29.529 --> 00:03:34.909
But to make these slides permanent, the microscopist
has to do things a little differently.

00:03:34.909 --> 00:03:40.420
Again, these techniques can vary, but if we
were to follow one set of instructions we

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:46.040
found to prepare a permanent diatom sample,
we’d start by allowing our watery microbe

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:52.819
mix to settle on a coverslip until the thin
layer of water has evaporated away.

00:03:52.819 --> 00:03:59.089
Then we would add a mounting medium to the
slide and turn the dry, diatom-coated coverslip

00:03:59.089 --> 00:04:05.969
onto it, allowing the medium to seep in and
preserve the organisms left behind.

00:04:05.969 --> 00:04:10.049
We are definitely simplifying this process
for narrative effect.

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:15.739
But we want to note that because of the volatile
compounds in the mounting process, the instructions

00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:22.590
also contain a specific note to perform this procedure in a fume hood (among other safety precautions).

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:27.190
So we recommend that anyone seeking to make
these prepared slides do a bit more research

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.819
on the best and safest methods for their needs.

00:04:30.819 --> 00:04:35.910
You may have noticed already that the diatoms
we are watching in these prepared slides bear

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:42.080
a strong resemblance to the living diatoms we’ve watched countless times on this channel before.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:48.710
But they’re also a bit different, with the
colors and shapes shifted just slightly, as

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.099
if we’re watching them through a filter.

00:04:51.099 --> 00:04:56.759
This is likely due to the use of acid to remove
their organic parts, erasing much of their

00:04:56.759 --> 00:05:03.030
biological contents from our view, but leaving
behind the stunning silicon frustules that

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:05.720
diatoms encase themselves in.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:11.660
The result feels a bit like roaming through
the dioramas in a natural history museum,

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:17.560
with scenes from nature rendered on the other
side of the glass to suggest a living world

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:24.550
that does not actually live.Those dioramas
have the stillness and sense of distance that

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:31.360
a photograph does, but they are also their
own direct experience that is shaped by the

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.710
setting you observe them in.

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:42.300
In that same way, these permanent slides are
their own unique perspective of a static microcosmos,

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:52.550
informed as much by the people who made them
as they are by their later observers.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:58.770
And these slides have been letting microscopists
preserve and share the objects of their fascination

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.540
for centuries.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:07.280
Around the mid-1800s, microscopy became kind
of a thing—particularly in Britain.

00:06:07.289 --> 00:06:13.340
Manufacturers were producing more affordable
microscopes, and users were publishing guides

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:18.550
to help others use this tool to navigate the
unseen world.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:24.520
Amateur and professional microscopists began
to form groups like the Quekett Microscopical

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:29.300
Club, united by the curiosities on the other
side of the lens.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:36.169
The slides they prepared carried everything
from beard hairs to insect exoskeletons to diatoms.

00:06:36.169 --> 00:06:42.539
And more than a century later, people still
collect these slides, studying their contents

00:06:42.539 --> 00:06:50.580
not just to observe the worlds mounted within them, but also to understand the people who made them.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:56.979
And just as art museums have to verify and
conserve the works produced by old masters,

00:06:56.979 --> 00:07:02.099
museums and collectors have to handle these
microscope slides with care.

00:07:02.099 --> 00:07:07.120
As one collector noted, these slides can sometimes
sell for thousands of dollars, attracting

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:13.320
forgers whose false work becomes apparent
in fonts that are too modern and cuts that

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:14.460
are too uneven.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:20.189
And the authentic slides require maintenance
as well, whether their surfaces need to be

00:07:20.189 --> 00:07:24.139
cleaned of grime or their contents re-preserved.

00:07:24.139 --> 00:07:31.819
The fascination and attention these centuries-old
slides inspire feels like a natural extension

00:07:31.819 --> 00:07:36.479
of microscopy both as a hobby and a scientific
pursuit.

00:07:36.479 --> 00:07:41.949
We’ve heard from many of you that the footage
we share on this show has made you want to

00:07:41.949 --> 00:07:47.850
buy a microscope, to sample the world around
you to see what it contains.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:53.960
And what are these slides except just another
way microscopists have been sharing the natural

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:55.590
world with each other?

00:07:55.590 --> 00:08:01.629
These slides don’t show us the microcosmos
as it once existed as much as they reflect

00:08:01.629 --> 00:08:09.159
the journey the microcosmos itself has taken, through solvents and hands and elements.

00:08:09.159 --> 00:08:11.819
Through microscopists and collectors.

00:08:11.819 --> 00:08:16.180
And then, of course, to you.

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:21.699
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:21.699 --> 00:08:26.080
And thank you especially to all of the people
here on the screen right now who are some

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:32.610
of our supporters on Patreon, where we now
have a $2 tier that includes access to a Journey

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.219
to the Microcosmos Discord.

00:08:34.219 --> 00:08:37.770
And if you want to learn more about that you
